    "contact_source_ids": [],
    "description": "The Ilkhanate was a Mongol dynasty governing Iran and neighboring territories from the mid-thirteenth century. Its history combines conquest, continued Mongol political traditions, conversion to Islam, and influential artistic patronage before unified rule disintegrated after Abu Sa'id's death in 1335. [s1][s2]",
    "tags": ["Medieval Iran", "Ilkhanids", "Mongol history", "Persian art", "Soltaniyeh"],
    "status": "Historical dynasty; no longer ruling",
    "editorial_status": "ready_for_review",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-26T12:15:52Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-26T12:15:52Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Ilkhanate: Mongol Rule and Cultural Change in Iran | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "Explore the Ilkhanate's origins, changing religious politics, artistic exchanges, monumental architecture, and final fragmentation."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "founding-and-conquest",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "A Mongol dynasty took shape in Iran",
        "content": "The Ilkhanate emerged from Hülegü's mid-thirteenth-century expedition into western Asia. Hülegü was a grandson of Chinggis Khan and brother of Möngke, the Great Khan. His arrival transformed an existing, comparatively loose Mongol presence into a more substantial dynastic establishment. Historians nevertheless debate how far establishing a permanent dynasty formed part of his original instructions. [s1]\n\nThe seizure of Baghdad in 1258 ended the Abbasid caliphate there. Ilkhanid power centered in northwestern Iran but extended across neighboring territories, making this a regional empire rather than a state confined to today's Iranian borders. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "religion-and-authority",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Conversion changed the court without erasing its past",
        "content": "Ghazan's conversion to Islam in 1295 marked a major political and religious transition. Muslim institutions gained royal support, yet Mongol ideas of sovereignty and connections with the Great Khan continued. Conversion should therefore not be described as an instantaneous replacement of one political culture by another. [s1]\n\nFormal rulership also differed from practical control. During Abu Sa'id's early reign, the commander Choban and his family exercised extensive authority across the provinces. The ruler's name at the top of the hierarchy alone does not explain who governed. [s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "art-and-exchange",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "New connections reshaped artistic production",
        "content": "Conquest and artistic patronage belong to the same history, but they describe different experiences. The Metropolitan Museum identifies intensified exchange with East Asia in manuscript painting and decorative arts. Cloud bands, floral motifs, and new treatments of pictorial space entered an existing Persian and Islamic repertoire. Illustrated books, including copies of the Shahnameh, the Persian Book of Kings, became important vehicles for this experimentation. [s2]\n\nThese developments were not confined to one medium. Paper and textiles helped designs circulate, while pottery, metalwork, and architectural decoration offered other surfaces for related visual ideas. The resulting artistic language continued under successor patrons after political unity ended. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "soltaniyeh",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Soltaniyeh makes the period tangible",
        "content": "The mausoleum of Öljaitü at Soltaniyeh, in Zanjan province, is a surviving expression of Ilkhanid architectural ambition. UNESCO highlights its octagonal form, turquoise-blue dome, and importance in the development of the double-shelled dome in Iran. The monument connects the dynasty's political history with construction techniques and decoration that visitors can still examine. [s4]\n\nPrecise construction dates differ between the inspected institutional accounts: UNESCO gives 1302–12, while the Metropolitan Museum gives 1307–13. The broad early-fourteenth-century dating is secure here; the narrower discrepancy remains explicit rather than being silently resolved. [s2][s4]",
        "source_ids": ["s2", "s4"]
      },
      {
        "id": "fragmentation",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "An end to unity, not to cultural influence",
        "content": "Abu Sa'id broke Choban's dominance in 1327, demonstrating how personal alliances shaped imperial government. His own death in 1335 was followed by competing claimants and the disintegration of unified rule. [s1][s3]\n\nThis explains apparently conflicting end dates. Political histories commonly emphasize 1335, whereas museum accounts may extend the Ilkhanid artistic period into the mid-fourteenth century. A dynasty's political collapse did not terminate its workshops, visual conventions, or influence on later rulers. [s1][s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2", "s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "selected-chronology",
        "type": "markdown-mermaid",
        "title": "Conquest, religious transition, and fragmentation",
        "content": "```mermaid\nflowchart TD\n  A[\"Mid-thirteenth century: Hülegü establishes dynastic power\"] -->|\"Chronological sequence\"| B[\"1258: Baghdad captured\"]\n  B -->|\"Chronological sequence\"| C[\"1295: Ghazan converts to Islam\"]\n  C -->|\"Chronological sequence\"| D[\"1327: Choban loses power\"]\n  D -->|\"Chronological sequence\"| E[\"1335: Abu Sa'id dies; unified rule fragments\"]\n```",
        "summary": "The sequence identifies selected political and religious turning points. Arrows indicate time order; they do not imply that conversion caused fragmentation or that artistic production ended in 1335. [s1][s2][s3]",
        "as_of_date": null,
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2", "s3"]
      }
    ],
